     coining "hash" in its computing sense, and his 1963 paper "File Organization and Addressing" became a
     canonical reference.

     The 1960s brought theoretical rigor. Donald Knuth, in The Art of Computer Programming, Volume 3: Sorting and
     Searching (1973), devoted a long, celebrated chapter to hashing, analyzing collision resolution mathematically.
      Before that, binary searching was the default for ordered data; hashing offered average-case constant-time
     operations, a dramatic departure.

     Collision handling developed along two great branches. Open addressing, where a colliding key probes other
     slots, was explored by Peterson and refined by Amdahl; separate chaining, where each bucket holds a list,
     became the workhorse of implementations from Lisp to Java. In 1968, Robert Morris described the now-famous
     folding and division methods, and the Fibonacci hashing trick emerged as a way to scatter keys cheaply.

     The 1970s and 1980s saw hashing mature. Universal hashing, introduced by Carter and Wegman in 1979, gave a
     randomized guarantee against adversarial worst cases — a profound theoretical advance. Linear probing received
     deeper analysis; coalesced hashing, cuckoo hashing (2001), and Robin Hood hashing explored new trade-offs in
     probe length and clustering.

     Practical software followed. Hash tables became fixtures of programming languages: Lisp's equal hash tables,
     Perl's hashes (1987), Python's dict (1991), Java's HashMap, Ruby's hashes, and JavaScript's objects and later
     Map. Each brought engineering lessons — Python's dict became a symbol of "the best default data structure,"
     with its 2016 redesign incorporating compact insertion-ordered arrays.

     Hashing also underpinned advances far beyond lookup tables: cryptographic hashing (MD5, SHA family) for
     integrity, consistent hashing (Karger et al., 1997) for distributed systems and load balancing, Bloom filters (
     1970) for probabilistic membership, and Merkle trees (1979) for verifying large datasets. Modern distributed
     databases, caches, and blockchains all rest on the same primitive.

